This paper investigates the existence of solutions for the following boundary value problem:
\begin{equation}
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
-\text{div}\left(a(x)\left| \frac{\left| \nabla u\right|}{|x|^\gamma}\right|^{p(x)-2}\nabla u\right|^{p(x)-2}\nabla u\right|=
\frac{a(x)a(u)}{|x|^\gamma}\text{div}\left(a(x)\left| \frac{\left| \nabla u\right|}{|x|^\gamma}\right|^{p(x)-q(x)-1}\nabla u\right|^{p(x)-q(x)}+f(x,u)\text{div}x \\
\text{in } \Omega:=\Bbb R^N\setminus B(0,1), \\
u=0\text{ on } \partial \Omega.
\end{array}\right.
\end{equation}
We introduce an adapted functional and use the mountain pass theorem to demonstrate the existence of a weak non-trivial solution.
